A||N||8||FieroSTETZ||finally drove the fiero w/ new engine - some questions||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||05-11-2001||03:10 AM||||i replaced the knocking engine in my 87 gt w/ an aluminum 2.8/3.1, and finally got to drive it today.. nice... the engine feels tight, but that should go away as it breaks in. it does run pretty hot though, even with the ac turned on, it rides just below halfway on the gauge no matter what, and seems to be burning oil, as well as hunting for an idle (wavers by about 500 rpm while still) its got a rodded out stock radiator, all new everything (broken egr, ran outta funds so i patched it up)new 190 thermostat - i don't mind it getting that warm, but it does it in like 2 minutes, what should i do?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||05-11-2001||08:53 AM||||Alluminum is a light metal, it'll heat up fast (much faster then cast iron) but it will also disapate that heat better. I wouldn't be to worried about the heat unless it's ridding into the red, and you may want to burp the coolant. Also, make sure the timing is acurate, that could account for the hunting idle, and the patched EGR would be something to replace.<P>just out of curiosity, I tought GM only made alluminum blocks for the 3.4. while I can understand putting a 2.8 crank in it, after blowing that much on a block (aren't they like $3000?) why not just save the exta $100-200 for the 3.1 crank and have a 3.4?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||05-11-2001||11:18 AM||||The oil burning should go away after the engine is broken in as long as you didn't use chrome rings and sleeves.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WampusCat||05-11-2001||11:29 AM||||My wife's car has an Aluminum 4cyl with turbo and it reaches operating temp in about a minute even in winter.  That's pretty normal for Al.  I'm still not used to how long it takes the iron 2.8 to warm up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroSTETZ||05-11-2001||11:46 AM||||the dude owed me a favor and i got a great deal - <P>i am using regular oil for the break in, how long before i should change to mobil one?||||reg||4
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-11-2001||11:56 AM||||This may not seem obvious but the purpose of the thermostat is to keep the engine at its rated temp, not to keep it below the temp.<P>So if you have a 190° stat in there, it should be at 190° just about all the time, if the engine is below 190 the stat will close down and restrict coolant flow till it reaches 190.<P>You want that! engines are not effecient when running cool. If you think 190 is too hot put a 180 or 160 stat inthere<P>ALSO, when you put the AC on the engine runs hotter, not cooler - worst case for the engine is driving in stop and go traffic with the AC on (frequent acceleration and not much air flow over the radiator, and the AC puts about a 4 HP additional load on the engine).<P>the engine runs coolest when you are cruising about 60mph with the heater on full blast (acts like a secondary radiator and cools the engine a little more).<P>the temp gauge on the dash is most likely to be off and reading on the low side.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-11-2001||12:10 PM||||dont forget too you have to drive about 30 miles at moderate speeds (not full acceleration) for the ECM to stabilize itself and zero in on the proper settings for the new engine.||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||05-11-2001||12:13 PM||||Ken - While your explanation for cooling w/ AC is true for most cars, that is not my experience with Fieros.  I live in WPB FL (warm) and my car seems to stay cooler by running the AC because the fan keeps airflow running thru the cores.  When the fan isn't on and I'm cruising, too much air pressure builds behind the radiator so less air is actually flowing thru it.  Maybe this is just my car though.||||reg||3
Z||000008||Dave Gunsul||05-11-2001||10:19 PM||||I am sooo jealous! I wanted that alum. block<BR>but the cost was way to high.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>BTW the alum. GM block is NOT a 3.4 block.<BR>It comes with the same 3.50 bore that the 2.8<BR>and 3.1 does.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
